WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.515
Before using AI on a codebase, define the
review scope.

2
00:00:05.595 --> 00:00:12.462
Scope identifies which repositories,
branches, components, languages,

3
00:00:12.542 --> 00:00:19.192
frameworks, services, and deployment paths
are authorized for review.

4
00:00:19.272 --> 00:00:22.651
It also identifies what is excluded.

5
00:00:22.731 --> 00:00:30.144
Exclusions matter because they prevent the
review from drifting into unrelated

6
00:00:30.224 --> 00:00:35.130
systems, third-party code, experimental
branches,

7
00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:40.006
or sensitive areas that require separate
approval.

8
00:00:40.086 --> 00:00:46.845
A clear scope turns the review from an
open-ended scan into an accountable

9
00:00:46.925 --> 00:00:49.105
engineering activity.

10
00:00:49.185 --> 00:00:52.783
Scope should include the review goals.

11
00:00:52.863 --> 00:00:59.404
A review may focus on authentication,
authorization, input handling,

12
00:00:59.484 --> 00:01:07.005
dependency risk, cryptography, secrets
exposure, file handling, API behavior,

13
00:01:07.085 --> 00:01:10.356
or a recent change in a pull request.

14
00:01:10.436 --> 00:01:16.650
The goal affects what evidence matters and
what prompts are useful.

15
00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:20.545
It also keeps the AI interaction focused.

16
00:01:20.625 --> 00:01:28.256
Asking for every possible problem in an
entire repository tends to produce shallow

17
00:01:28.336 --> 00:01:29.099
output.

18
00:01:29.179 --> 00:01:36.592
Asking targeted questions against a scoped
component produces results that are

19
00:01:36.672 --> 00:01:38.307
easier to verify.

20
00:01:38.387 --> 00:01:41.330
AI usage rules are part of scope.

21
00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:48.496
Decide whether the review uses a local
model, a private enterprise AI system,

22
00:01:48.576 --> 00:01:50.320
or a cloud service.

23
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.396
Decide what code, configuration, logs,
secrets, customer data,

24
00:01:56.476 --> 00:02:00.073
and proprietary details can be shared.

25
00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:05.059
Sensitive files may need to be excluded or
sanitized.

26
00:02:05.139 --> 00:02:12.879
The reviewer should know the data handling
expectations before sending any content

27
00:02:12.959 --> 00:02:14.376
to an AI system.

28
00:02:14.456 --> 00:02:22.305
Good scope protects the organization while
still giving the reviewer enough context

29
00:02:22.385 --> 00:02:24.020
to do useful work.
